Prefer a union-typed parameter when the function body works the same way for both types. This matches the TypeScript handbook's guidance in the "Do's and Don'ts" and the section on writing good overloads: if you can write the function with a union, don't write overloads.

```ts
function len(x: string | any[]) {
  return x.length;
}
```

This is better because callers can pass a value whose type is itself `string | any[]`, which fails with overloads:

```ts
function len(s: string): number;
function len(arr: any[]): number;
function len(x: any) { return x.length; }

declare const v: string | any[];
len(v); // error: no overload matches this call
```

The implementation signature isn't visible to callers, so a union argument can't resolve to either overload.

Use overloads when the return type depends on which input type was passed, or when the parameter combinations are related in ways a union can't express. For example:

```ts
function first(x: string): string;
function first<T>(x: T[]): T;
```

If you still want overloads and need to accept unions, you can add a third overload for the union, but at that point the union-typed version is usually simpler.

A related tip from the same guidance: when the return type can be expressed by a generic or conditional type, that is often cleaner than a stack of overloads.

