You get the error because of TypeScript's excess property checking. Object literals get special treatment and undergo excess property checking when assigned to other variables or passed as arguments. If an object literal has any properties that the "target type" doesn't have, you'll get an error. Here, `colour` isn't in `SquareConfig`, so the call is rejected.

The reasoning is that TypeScript takes the stance that there's probably a bug in this code. In plain JavaScript, this sort of misspelling fails silently.

**Ways to fix or get around it:**

- **Fix the typo:** In most cases this is the right answer, since a majority of excess property errors are actually bugs.
- **Type assertion:** The easiest method to get around the checks is to use a type assertion, e.g. `{ width: 100, opacity: 0.5 } as SquareConfig`.
