answers.text:
No. With `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` enabled, you can't assign `undefined` to an optional property unless `undefined` is explicitly part of its declared type. The flag specifies that optional property types should be interpreted exactly as written, meaning that `| undefined` is not added to the type.

By default, the opposite is true. TypeScript doesn't distinguish between a present property with the value `undefined` and a missing property.

Example from the docs:

```ts
interface Person {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
}

// With 'exactOptionalPropertyTypes' on:
const p: Person = {
  name: "Daniel",
  age: undefined, // Error! undefined isn't a number
};
```

The same applies to assignments after creation. With the flag on, `settings.colorThemeOverride = "dark"` and `"light"` are allowed, but `settings.colorThemeOverride = undefined` is not.

Also note that this flag is not part of the `strict` family and must be turned on explicitly. It also requires `strictNullChecks` to be enabled.

